<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Congo River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Congo River is the second longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> to the Atlantic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a> occupied the lands south of its lower course. The river's mouth was reached by the Portuguese in the 1480s, leading to the Kongo Empire's contact with Portugal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/congo-river.jpg" alt="Congo River" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MONUSCO/Myriam Asmani · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aerial_view_of_the_Congo_River_near_Kisangani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Congo River, formerly also called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a>, is the only major river to cross the equator twice. Its basin covers about 4 million square kilometres and ten countries, around 13 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. The Chambeshi River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> is generally taken as its source, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba</a> is the river upstream of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boyoma-falls">Boyoma Falls</a>. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisangani">Kisangani</a> it flows north-west, then south-west to Pool Malebo, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazzaville">Brazzaville</a> face each other. Below the Pool it falls through the Livingstone Falls, a series of 32 falls and rapids, to the sea at Muanda. Its flow is stable because its basin lies on both sides of the Equator, and it is one of the deepest rivers in the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The whole basin is populated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu peoples</a>, whose expansion is estimated to have reached the middle Congo by about 500 BCE. The name Congo comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>, once on the southern bank, formed in the late 14th century. The name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a> is a Portuguese adaptation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a> nzere, river. European exploration began in 1482, when the Portuguese explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a> found the estuary and made contact with the Kingdom of Kongo. The full course remained unknown throughout the early modern period.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a>'s expedition of 1876-77 proved that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lualaba">Lualaba</a> fed the Congo and not the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>. It reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boma">Boma</a> on 9 August 1877, having most probably lost 132 people to disease, hunger, drowning, killing and desertion. Stanley founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> as a trading post in 1881, named Léopoldville for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, who claimed the basin privately as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> in 1885.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Republic of the Congo</a> are both named after the river. Although the Livingstone Falls block access from the sea, nearly the whole river above them is navigable in sections, especially between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Kinshasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisangani">Kisangani</a>, and it remains a lifeline in a land with few roads or railways. Its basin holds the Congo rainforest, the second-largest rainforest area in the world, and almost 800 fish species have been recorded there, many found nowhere else.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Congo River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Henry Morton Stanley completed his descent of the <b>Congo River</b> in 1877, Leopold hired him, and from 1879 to 1884 Stanley built stations along the river, including Vivi and Léopoldville, and gathered hundreds of treaties by which Congolese chiefs ceded land and rights to Leopold’s organisations, which…</blockquote><a href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Their trade reached the Teke Kingdom and the <b>Congo River</b> beyond, and by the late eighteenth century slaves from the Bobangi area were a significant share of exports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Kongo arose in the late fourteenth century in the region south of the lower <b>Congo River</b>, where it brought a series of Bantu-speaking communities into a centralised polity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1880 Pierre Savorgnan de Brazza signed a treaty with the Makoko, ruler of the Kingdom of Tio, placing land north of the <b>Congo River</b> under French protection; Brazzaville grew from his post.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-congo">French Congo</a></li><li><blockquote>Kakongo was one of the Kikongo-speaking kingdoms north of the <b>Congo</b> estuary that recognised the senior Kingdom of Loango in early times.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kakongo">Kingdom of Kakongo</a></li><li><blockquote>Ngoyo was one of three Kikongo-speaking kingdoms on the coast north of the <b>Congo</b> estuary, alongside the Kingdom of Loango and the Kingdom of Kakongo.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ngoyo">Kingdom of Ngoyo</a></li></ul></section>
